bin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3=\\left(\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}\n$$\n\nNow, computing $(-3)^3=-27$ and $2^3=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}= \\dfrac{-27}{8}=-\\dfrac{27}{8} .\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186105742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478377 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186105743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478380 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186105744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478376 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186105744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478425 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186105965 1050 420.0 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186106469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186106469 1071 "$(4÷11)^{3}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186106469 1071 "What is $(4 \\div 11)^{3}$?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186106469 1074 "To raise the quotient of two numbers to the power of $3$, we must raise each number to the power of $3,$ and then divide:\n\n$$\n(4÷11)^{3}={4}^{3}÷{11}^{3}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186106472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186106472 1071 "$6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186106472 1071 "What is $6(\\frac{2}{3})^{3}$?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186106472 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=6\\cdot \\frac{8}{27}=\\frac{48}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{27}=\\frac{16\\cdot 3}{9\\cdot 3}=\\frac{16}{9}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186107083 1064 "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3\n$$\n\nby applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{2}{5})^{3} &= (\\frac{2}{5}) \\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{3}}{5^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186107083 1064 "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3$ by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 2}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119605 1071 "Given that\n![](images/ae/aea6bc2396725e1f24bbd196f15c590fbd9628dd15f220bc57653bae25840773.svg) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119605 1071 "Given that\n![](images/d4/d4fa85cab74f4bda57059d1c6abc9dacea665531ce84ba9d2f1598a13c49ef60.png) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186119606 1074 "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](images/5e/5eb696ca679a0079e8460d472b6f172df4f2e29f5dfef6cbdee388c55dcb1db5.png)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ F & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186119606 1074 "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](images/a9/a99ebe6a4021d9124320439383f1e5351f134a321a353c2dafaa7bbf8555aae7.svg)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ F & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119607 1071 "Given that\n![](images/c2/c21c2b99cdd8480ab891a23f92687d29230da7da9de9bd75a903919e54c48a1f.svg)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119607 1071 "Given that\n![](images/e8/e85dac75798b6cb463403f54ab2a675206d7eeea0a8958081aac9c696047b345.png)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186119760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478445 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478379 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478418 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478391 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478403 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478404 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478406 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478385 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478389 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186119796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478435 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186120002 1050 900.0 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186120190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117301 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186120362 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=4{b}_{1}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186120362 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=4{b}_{1}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186121029 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186121029 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186124337 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186124337 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nI. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\nII. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\nIII. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124338 1071 "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\n- $B +4I$ is singular\n\n- $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186124338 1071 "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\nII. $B +4I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124338 1074 "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/60/6031351ba073c1da6df2d7e96ef1764eff8d6e15d1d1c388e06edf68dc6e9083.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124338 1074 "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/62/6273b311b461cc16f516a4d9da6d928643e988c7847cfd14e23401a265d9e25b.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186124417 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186124417 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124417 1074 "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/08/084da4d2f1ef5b0a2359a9a6b7582daf57f619842453fd911233eb004862b941.svg)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124417 1074 "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/fd/fd48d2627c161d3e82529aeeb42bcd830c2fc83a9c8d0b7522d1857f099d6f38.png)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186124528 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478441 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124529 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478454 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124642 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478455 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186124751 1050 420.0 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186125789 1064 "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$* \n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186125789 1064 "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n> *An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131174 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131175 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131176 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131177 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186131178 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477799 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477856 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477875 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477901 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186131173 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478001 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186131173 1078 #edb/ref 17592186477875 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133552 1071 "Consider ![](images/46/4632b2999b4578282c7f0d71fdf36004123551187fa9a6980051d7ff89f48296.svg) and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133552 1071 "Consider ![](images/ba/ba3f1852b12b6f4a246a855c88c9b867b7e750c6dddf350cab0324629fc047c4.png) and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186133553 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation $R_2 := R_2-2R_1$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/8d/8d015aca8950073c165d53b14f86f0e65a207438ad901ce82f85f65bb8347519.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186133553 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation $R_2 := R_2-2R_1$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/a2/a23e35f367c6d8ca4bdbee47bacf9afca23dea031b0a647dda5067746bf45e46.svg)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133554 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is $R_1:=6R_1.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/37/375f1950c460b1f310fa279f6d7f203f88ff641000715dfaec192f47d785aa93.png)\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A,$ we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/63/6357cc2a2526f38a0f0f7e2e7ef4ee7a1a564adfa70264028ff732f44ba7b763.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186133554 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is $R_1:=6R_1.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d2/d2425878812bb00748c5250dd877655a7d01faab33e148593be2a6c2de98ac4e.svg)\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A,$ we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c7/c741f5faf39a8bc1606d64e906162bbd5b67b3dfd88af2372f43e96736de6bb5.svg)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478375 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478461 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478453 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478383 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186133980 1050 420.0 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186134189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186134189 1071 "Consider $E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and a $3 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$. Find the row operation that, when applied to $A$, is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186134189 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186134189 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the second row of ${I}_{3}$ by $-\\frac{2}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186135024 1064 "Applying a row operation to any $3\\times 3$ matrix is the same as *pre-multiplying* (multiplying on the left) the matrix by the corresponding elementary matrix.\n\nFor instance, if we wish to swap the first row and the second row of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ we pre-multiply $A$ by the corresponding elementary matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nto get $E_1 A.$\n\nTo perform a sequence of row operations to a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we pre-multiply the matrix by the elementary matrix corresponding to the first operation, and then pre-multiply the result by the elementary matrix corresponding to the second operation.\n\nFor instance, given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ suppose that we would like to multiply the second row by $2$ and then add $3$ times the first row to the second row. To do this, we first pre-multiply $A$ by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nto get $E_2 A.$ Then we pre-multiply the result by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nto get $E_3 E_2 A.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 fa