minator of $4$ by splitting the shape on the right into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/c4/c401472d271b22b1461c6f02888196a29b6b5b1d069d52638c325e807caab8c0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{4}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n3÷2=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143316 1071 "$\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143316 1071 "What is $\\frac{1}{3} \\div \\frac{1}{6}$?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186143316 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{1}{6}$ is $\\frac{6}{1}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1}=\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1}=\\frac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n6÷3=2\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143317 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{7}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143317 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$\\frac{4}{7} \\div \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{0}{7}$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186143317 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{5}$ is $\\frac{5}{2}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{4\\times 5}{7\\times 2}=\\frac{20}{14}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{14}=\\frac{20÷2}{14÷2}=\\frac{10}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $10.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143332 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{8}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143332 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$\\frac{3}{8} \\div \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{0}{8}$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186143332 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{3}{5}$ is $\\frac{5}{3}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{3\\times 5}{8\\times 3}=\\frac{15}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{24}=\\frac{15÷3}{24÷3}=\\frac{5}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $5.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143548 1071 "![](images/57/57382cd8235f5b141e5414492d3375178da41136f547620b1dd7acfc664b158b.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\frac{1}{4} \\div \\frac{3}{4}$." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186143548 1071 "![](images/57/57382cd8235f5b141e5414492d3375178da41136f547620b1dd7acfc664b158b.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186143548 1074 "![](images/24/24e777c7b9029007debe066de66ea7041b96d02702788bfea4d2da4914a47da6.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{3}{4}$ fits into $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $3$ fits into $1.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}$, we can simply compute $1÷3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n1÷3=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186144078 1064 "To divide two fractions, we multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nThe second fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nand the reciprocal of this fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 3 4 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {4} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} = \\dfrac {3 \\times 5} {4 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nSolving this problem using fraction models gives the same result:\n\n![](images/de/de4e554717f886b8e4b289a50d19e57f1b30383f0d7f4bf7b85476a57a442706.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186144078 1064 "To divide two fractions, we multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nThe second fraction is $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5},$ and the reciprocal of this fraction is $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 3 4 \\times  \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}  .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {4} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} =  \\dfrac {3 \\times 5} {4 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{15}{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving this problem using fraction models gives the same result:\n\n![](images/de/de4e554717f886b8e4b289a50d19e57f1b30383f0d7f4bf7b85476a57a442706.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186145346 1078 #edb/ref 17592186145347 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186147173 1071 "Evaluate $4^2 +(5-3)$." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186147173 1071 "Evaluate $4^2 +(5-3).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186147173 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n4^2\\,+\\underbrace{(5-3)}_{\\large\\color{blue} 2}  =4^2\\,+ {\\color{blue} 2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{4^2}^{\\large\\color{red} 16}+2 = {\\color{red} 16}+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n16+2 = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4^2 +(5-3)=18.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186147173 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n4^2\\,+\\underbrace{(5-3)}_{\\large\\color{blue} 2} =4^2\\,+ {\\color{blue} 2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{4^2}^{\\large\\color{red} 16}+2 = {\\color{red} 16}+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n16+2 = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4^2 +(5-3)=18\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186147174 1071 "Calculate $28\\div(7-5)^2$." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186147174 1071 "Calculate $28\\div(7-5)^2.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186147174 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P*