186185557 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186185562 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185564 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186185658 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185662 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188002 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n![](images/c7/c73c9ecb4b2f4288cdefd5546f8ec21f46008695568318e9344eb70c88ffa334.svg)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f_Y(y) = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$ for $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$\n\nII. $f(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188002 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n${\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_Y(y) = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$ for $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$\n2. $f(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188002 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y$, we get\n$f_{Y}(y)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}dx; = \\frac{1}{4}e^{-y/2}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2}; = \\frac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$,\nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X$, we get\n$f_{X}(x)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}dy; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{b}e^{-y/2}dy; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)[- 2e^{-y/2}]_{0}^{b}; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)[- 2e^{-b/2} + 2]; = \\frac{x}{2}$,\nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$. So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$, we obtain that\n$\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188002 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-y/2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-y/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-2{e}^{-y/2}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-2{e}^{-b/2}+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right].$ So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right),$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188003 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/f5/f5e1e2931a6ad6c4b5751d67e325cc0aca6cbae85200e5d2fbd6f60ea28c899c.svg)\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188003 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n${\\frac{4}{x^{3}y^{3}},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1, y \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind the expected value of $Y$." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188003 1074 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when\n\n$$\ny \\geq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{4}{x^{3}y^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}lim_(b → ∞)\\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{2x^{2}}]_{1}^{b} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}lim_(b → ∞)(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2b^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{y^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{y^{3}},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nFinally, the expected value of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y \\cdot f_{Y}(y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{∞}y \\cdot \\frac{2}{y^{3}}dy \\\\\n&= 2lim_(b → ∞)\\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{y}]_{1}^{b} \\\\\n&= 2lim_(b → ∞)(1 - \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188003 1074 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when $y \\geq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n![](images/4d/4dcb0094faeee6f3f155eb2e7a87c24cfb976c3f860f30b13cd7da61512544b2.svg)\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }y\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188209 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/ecb3e3a465f5987f182ccf843562a43f1e4c65db0a7b12e8e86cd9724dfa2d7b.svg)\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188209 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n${\\frac{2}{9}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188209 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\