Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{3x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{5y}{6x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in \\left[2,5\\right]\\times \\left[1,3\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5y}{6x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5, 1 \\le y \\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 3, Y \\le 2) &= \\int_{3}^{5}[\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{5y}{6x^{2}}dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6x^{2}}[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6x^{2}}(\\frac{3}{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{4x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{5}{4x}]_{3}^{5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188210 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5y}{6{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y) \\in \\left[2,5\\right]\\times \\left[1,3\\right].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n![](images/eb/eb900092e22a38e1bbc0b26853647353d5d21fc0f46c56842525e9b11d5392a1.svg)\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{5y}{6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6{x}^{2}}(\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4x}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188211 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\textrm{E}[4X-3Y]$.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188211 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\textrm{E}[4X-3Y].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188211 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,  the probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0.35+2\\cdot 0.35+4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =-0.35+0.7+1.2 \\\\  & =1.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.2+3\\cdot 0.2+5\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0+0.2+0.6+1.5 \\\\  & =2.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4X-3Y] & =4\\text{E}[X]-3\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4(1.55)-3(2.3) \\\\  & =6.2-6.9 \\\\  & =-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188211 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n| $x$$-1$$2$$4$$f_X(x)$$0.35$$0.35$$0.3$ |  | $y$$0$$1$$3$$5$$f_Y(y)$$0.3$$0.2$$0.2$$0.3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}f_{X}(x_{i}) \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot 0.35 + 2 \\cdot 0.35 + 4 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&=-0.35 + 0.7 + 1.2 \\\\\n&= 1.55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i}f_{Y}(y_{i}) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.3 + 1 \\cdot 0.2 + 3 \\cdot 0.2 + 5 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0 + 0.2 + 0.6 + 1.5 \\\\\n&= 2.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[4X - 3Y] &= 4E[X] - 3E[Y] \\\\\n&= 4(1.55) - 3(2.3) \\\\\n&= 6.2 - 6.9 \\\\\n&=-0.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478387 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478414 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478431 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478444 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478381 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478394 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478396 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478423 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478424 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478400 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478429 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478433 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478440 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478421 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [1759218618829