center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+1\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0+0.6 \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =0+0.25+0.9 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-2Y] & =5\\,\\text{E}[X]-2\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5(0.6)-2(1.15) \\\\  & =3-2.3 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188546 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188546 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$f_{X}(x) = {3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{Y}(y) = {4y^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$.\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188546 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188547 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188547 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{3}{2}(x^{2} + y^{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{X}(x) = \\frac{3}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}$ for $x ∈ [0, 1]$\n2. $f(x, y) = f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188547 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188563 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=2x$ for $x\\in [2,4]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186188563 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{2}{3}xy^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 4, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{X}(x) = 2x$ for $x ∈ [2, 4]$\n2. $f(x, y) = f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186188563 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}(8-2) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [2,4]$ and $y\\in [0,1],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{2}{3}x{y}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\frac{1}{6}x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(4{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186189371 1064 "To compute the expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ given a joint mass function $f(x,y)$, we must first find its marginal mass function $f_X$. Once the marginal mass function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i \\, f_X(x_i)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Since we're only interested in the expected value of $X$, let's compute the row totals only.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.20}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.80}$ |\n\nTherefore, the marginal mass function of $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.80$ |\n\nWe can now compute the expected value of $X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}f_{X}(x_{i}) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 0.2 + 2 \\cdot 0.8 \\\\\n&= 0.2 + 1.6 \\\\\n&= 1.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = 1.8\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186189371 1064 "To compute the expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ given a joint mass function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal mass function $f_X.$ Once the marginal mass function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i \\, f_X(x_i).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Since we're only interested in the expected value of $X,$ let's compute the row totals only.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.20}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.80}$ |\n\nTherefore, the marginal mass function of $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.80$ |\n|  |  |  |\n\nWe can now compute the expected value of $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.2+1.6 \\\\  & =1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 1.8.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186189379 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb R$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\in A, Y\\in B)=P(X\\in A)\\cdot P(Y\\in B)\n$$\n\nIn practice, this is not a very helpful definition when checking for independence since it involves verifying that it's true for all possible subsets $A$ and $B$!\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$, and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal density functions of $X$ and $Y$, respectively. Then, it can be shown that $X$ and $Y$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$ and $y$. Notice that this has a direct analogy with the case of independence for discrete random variables." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186189379 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\in A, Y\\in B)=P(X\\in A)\\cdot P(Y\\in B).\n$$\n\nIn practice, this is not a very helpful definition when checking for independence since it involves verifying that it's true for all possible subsets $A$ and $B!$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal density functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Then, it can be shown that $X$ and $Y$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all values of $x$ and $y.$ Notice that this has a direct analogy with the case of independence for discrete random variables." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186189382 1064 "To compute the expected value of a *continuous* random variable $X$ given a joint density function $f(x,y)$, we must first find its marginal density function $f_X$. Once the marginal density function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x \\cdot f_X(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186189382 1064 "To compute the expected value of a *continuous* random variable $X$ given a joint density function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal density function $f_X.$ Once the marginal density function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty x \\cdot f_X(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186189419 1064 "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ whose joint support $S$ is rectangular, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose that the joint support of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is rectangular. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nfor some two functions $g$ and $h$ (not necessarily probability density functions), then $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Note that the rectangular joint support $S$ does not need to be finite.* \n\nFor example, let's consider the random variables $X$ and $Y$ with the following joint PDF:\n\n![](images/01/01a68d0e42bc71729543e74722390291787ce60530c2432ffa0cdf6c0bb5f093.svg)\n\nThe joint support $[0,2]\\times [0, \\infty)$ is rectangular. Also, we can write $f(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y),$ where\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac14 x, \\qquad h(y) = e^{-y/2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186189419 1064 "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ whose joint support $S$ is rectangular, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *Suppose that the joint support of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is rectangular. If\n> $f(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)$\n> for some two functions $g$ and $h$ (not necessarily probability density functions), then $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Note that the rectangular joint support $S$ does not need to be finite.*\n\nFor example, let's consider the random variables $X$ and $Y$ with the following joint PDF:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe joint support $[0,2]\\times [0, \\infty)$ is rectangular. Also, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac14 x, \\qquad h(y) = e^{-y/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent." 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186198431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478459 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186247306 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186247306 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186247309 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186247309 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186247309 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the equations, we get  \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2})-3(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{1}-12{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186247309 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the equations, we get  \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2})-3(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{1}-12{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186335847 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186335847 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186410061 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186410061 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-2{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 false] [17592186410062 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186410062 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \