] [17592186478368 1075 #edb/ref 17592186477943 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478368 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478120 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478368 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478171 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478368 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478368 1077 "field-3" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478368 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478120 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478368 1079 #uuid "ffa10310-b897-5f9d-8f7f-96575809586b" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048825 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048924 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049143 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049253 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049640 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049752 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050462 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1089 #edb/ref 17592186478370 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478369 1090 #uuid "ee683fb6-8dcb-5c70-bc16-77d5deb6c97d" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478468 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478474 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478477 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478489 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478492 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478496 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1084 #edb/ref 17592186478497 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1085 "A foundation is organizing a juice and cake sale for charity." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1086 #uuid "bda8c7b2-ecbe-5320-9b0d-e5ab48e508b8" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478370 1087 #edb/ref 17592186478492 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478304 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478371 1069 "q-62823" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478371 1071 "Air is being pumped into a rubber speed boat. The rate at which the pressure increases in the boat is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2t+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in PSI per second and $t>0$ is the time in seconds since the pumping started.\n\nThe boat has a slow puncture which releases air at a rate of $1$ PSI every second.\n\nIf the boat had a pressure of $10$ PSI in the beginning, how much pressure does it have after $20$ seconds?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478371 1072 #uuid "08b5b55f-08a9-5a88-9bb3-7419f5e97ed9" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478371 1074 "The pressure increases at a rate of $2t+4$ PSI per second and is decreasing at a rate of $1$ PSI per second. Therefore, the **rate of change of pressure** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-1 \\\\  & =2t+4-1 \\\\  & =2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total pressure** after $t$ seconds (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the quantity of pressure after $20$ seconds, we substitute $t=20$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(20) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{20}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}+3x]|}_{0}^{20} \\\\  & =10+[({20}^{2}+3(20))] \\\\  & =10+460 \\\\  & =470\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478261 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478372 1069 "q-196288" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478372 1071 "Expressed as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form, $\\frac{2}{3}÷\\frac{4}{9}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478372 1072 #uuid "0d4e147c-1e80-5935-ab97-075efc2ca0dc" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478372 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{4}{9}$ is $\\frac{9}{4}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}÷\\frac{4}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{2\\times 9}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{18}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{12}=\\frac{18÷6}{12÷6}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478353 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478373 1069 "q-337283" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478373 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=({x}^{2}-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478373 1072 #uuid "0da1f25f-2816-5ff2-a8b8-8be9cce0ae8f" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478373 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478271 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478374 1069 "q-149609" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478374 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{7}(x+\\sqrt{y}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{7},$ find $\\text{E}[2X+Y].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478374 1072 #uuid "0eaad2fb-faea-5de0-88f1-158f02ba672a" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478374 1074 "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6}{7}(x+\\sqrt{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x\\sqrt{y}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}(2\\sqrt{y}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3}{7}(2\\sqrt{y}+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{3/2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}{[\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{5/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}(\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X+Y] & =2\\,\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{7})+(\\frac{39}{70}) \\\\  & =\\frac{80+39}{70} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478337 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478375 1069 "q-75107" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478375 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478375 1072 #uuid "0f630631-31e9-5df2-8934-f227dd41371f" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478375 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the third row of ${I}_{3}$ by $\\frac{5}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{5}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{4}{R}_{3}$ to $A.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478299 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478376 1069 "q-68726" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478376 1071 "${(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478376 1072 #uuid "15ba712f-f7ba-599f-bc5d-e7c34fa5849d" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478376 1074 "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(-1)}{5}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${5}^{2}=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478250 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478377 1069 "q-68714" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478377 1071 "${(\\frac{5}{8})}^{9}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478377 1072 #uuid "15c54245-09aa-5df3-b591-faa8e2b79d79" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478377 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $9$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $9:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{9}=\\frac{{5}^{9}}{{8}^{9}}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478321 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478378 1069 "q-134041" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478378 1071 "Mark drank $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the amount of orange juice that he bought. What **exact** amount of juice did Mark drink?\n\n*Remember that Mark bought $\\frac{7}{4}$ liters of orange juice.*\n\nMark drank\n{{field-1}}\nliters of orange juice." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478378 1072 #uuid "17030b91-3cba-5c59-ac6a-c1eb417052bf" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478378 1074 "We know that Mark has $\\frac{7}{4}$ of a liter of orange juice.\n\nTo find the fraction of a liter that Mark drank, we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{2}$ by $\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 7}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike drank $\\frac{7}{8}$ liters of orange juice." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478345 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478379 1069 "q-75178" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478379 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}],$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478379 1072 #uuid "17c26653-7b83-5324-906b-245647d63511" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478379 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=2.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478350 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478380 1069 "q-68717" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478380 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478380 1072 #uuid "20ca6e4d-f7bc-55f3-af8a-939697faa8e0" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478380 1074 "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using, ${1}^{3}=1$ and ${4}^{3}=64,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{4}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{64}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478284 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478381 1069 "q-143361" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478381 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478381 1072 #uuid "22625d61-74d2-5702-9e7b-d31919b31ad0" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478381 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{7}{12}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{24}]}_{1}^{1.5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7(1.5)^{2}}{24}-\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.365.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478267 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478382 1069 "q-43544" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478382 1071 "Find $\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}$." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478382 1072 #uuid "227dea09-ff86-55c7-8478-5e0e49c51311" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478382 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2})(\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}) & = \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2})}^{3}{(\\sqrt{8})}^{2} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 8 & = \\\\ 16 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478247 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478383 1069 "q-110489" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478383 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:=(-1){R}_{3},\\,{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478383 1072 #uuid "22a37135-efbb-5d81-a4c1-6f681fabaf48" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478383 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{3}:=(-1){R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478356 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478384 1069 "q-99280" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478384 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt{23}\\cdot \\sqrt{23}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478384 1072 #uuid "26cb34dd-0a52-56c7-ad81-c3f0b8646304" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478384 1074 "First, we re-write the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{23}\\cdot \\sqrt{23}={(\\sqrt{23})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{23})}^{2}=23.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478357 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478385 1069 "q-75104" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478385 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478385 1072 #uuid "277999dd-79b5-5a59-931f-a7b78502d7ff" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478385 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{1}{2},{u}_{33}=-2,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=1.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478307 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478386 1069 "q-346823" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478386 1071 "A homeowner is tracking the rate at which electricity is being used in her house. The rate $r(t)$ at which electricity is consumed is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{2}-8t+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in kilowatts, and $0<t<6$ is the time in hours since $9\\,\\text{am}.$\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} kilowatt-hours were consumed." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478386 1072 #uuid "297141b4-8227-5679-ae9f-447866ab2753" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478386 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of electricity consumption $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of electricity consumed by the house between the times $t=0$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{t}^{2}-8t+10)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{8}{2}{t}^{2}+10t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+10t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}-4(3)^{2}+10(3))-(0-0+0) \\\\  & =18-36+30 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ hours, $12$ kilowatt-hours were consumed." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478253 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478387 1069 "q-143351" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478387 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478387 1072 #uuid "321896e4-7ca4-574a-a1b1-f3375d212e65" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478387 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478322 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478388 1069 "q-71375" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478388 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt[3]{-6}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{-6}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{-6}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478388 1072 #uuid "34230cac-29a3-5073-9c5a-a7d7f65982f6" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478388 1074 "First, we re-write the product using the cube:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{-6}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{-6}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{-6}={(\\sqrt[3]{-6})}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we use that  the cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt[3]{-6})}^{3}=-6.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478332 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478389 1069 "q-75103" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478389 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478389 1072 #uuid "36db6e8d-a408-5095-ac80-d2586ba2ff72" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478389 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-5,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-20.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478310 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478390 1069 "q-134035" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478390 1071 "Margaret and Ivana each bought $3$ blackberry muffins and $5$ strawberry muffins. In total, they bought$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$muffins." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478390 1072 #uuid "372b47e2-6ed6-5dd6-9cf6-680592eef643" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478390 1074 "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of muffins.\n\nSince each of them bought $3$ blackberry muffins and $5$ strawberry muffins, the number of muffins each of them bought is:\n\n$$\n{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blackberry muffins}}+{{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{strawberry muffins}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person bought this number of muffins, and there are $2$ people, we must multiply the number of muffins by $2.$ Therefore, the total number of muffins is:\n\n$$\n{{(3+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+5)\\times 2 & =(3+5)\\times 2 \\\\  & =8\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n8\\times 2=16\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478296 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478391 1069 "q-106834" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478391 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478391 1072 #uuid "3ab361a7-aba9-5ffc-b00a-8d8895886bc2" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478391 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=12.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478289 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478392 1069 "q-62813" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478392 1071 "The price of crude oil, measured in dollars per barrel, increases at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{2}{1+3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478392 1072 #uuid "3cbd327e-e7fe-5c08-8abb-efce49ecca7e" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478392 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of the oil price $r(t).$ To find the **total price** after $t$ months (call it $P(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478323 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478393 1069 "q-71371" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478393 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt{12}\\cdot \\sqrt{12}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478393 1072 #uuid "3e0e0b71-d0fc-56c1-a85f-87827e748262" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478393 1074 "First, we re-write the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{12}\\cdot \\sqrt{12}={(\\sqrt{12})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{12})}^{2}=12.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478313 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478394 1069 "q-143356" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478394 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478394 1072 #uuid "418cd51b-54c6-5702-b58c-50620816a1ae" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478394 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [2,4]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8+4)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{9}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478361 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478395 1069 "q-107575" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478395 1071 "$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478395 1072 #uuid "41ce9e80-1405-5642-831b-08af7169731b" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478395 1074 "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478331 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478396 1069 "q-143358" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478396 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}y, & \\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478396 1072 #uuid "427c86d9-8f85-5921-9341-2ec04eff93eb" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478396 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,4]\\times [2,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,2\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{20}xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{20}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478255 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478351 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478368 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1069 "q-337443" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1072 #uuid "46fed2b7-c806-57ef-a1e1-bd253db964cc" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478397 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478257 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478398 1069 "q-62214" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478398 1071 "$5\\times (1+{2}^{3})=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478398 1072 #uuid "47499e89-da8f-50c2-b67a-a2458e6c7f6f" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478398 1074 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $(1+{2}^{3}).$\n\n To evaluate $(1+{2}^{3})$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n1\\,+\\overset{8}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{3}}}=1+8\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n1+8=9\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times {{(1+{2}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{9}=5\\times 9\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times 9=45\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478258 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478399 1069 "q-90749" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478399 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478399 1072 #uuid "480dd21a-6ad7-5569-99ae-370c6e08eaac" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478399 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(8-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3(8-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =25+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+3{[8x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =25+3[(8\\cdot 4-{4}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =25+3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $25<73$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $73$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478365 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478400 1069 "q-143570" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478400 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[Y-X].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478400 1072 #uuid "48136ec2-d65d-59a7-87bb-a7d2f39b6298" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478400 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,  the probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5+1 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0.25+1\\cdot 0.15+2\\cdot 0.35+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =-0.25+0.15+0.7+1 \\\\  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y-X] & =\\text{E}[Y]-\\,\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =1.6-1.5 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478344 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478401 1069 "q-61395" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478401 1071 "![](images/10/108b7e775bdbd2422c87691b3ea68a82aa68ee925d5dc01517ac7327bba5959f.png)\n\nCalculate $\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3}$ using the model above." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478401 1072 #uuid "53eb3b3f-e5cc-5f81-bd02-ccecc2d8b701" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478401 1074 "The denominators are $3$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $3$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $4$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/1e/1e7a163bb4ee9efb6574e55a37ddeeac3503f2b2b873ba7ba253a969c92d3bb7.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{12}÷\\frac{8}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n9÷8=1\\,\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3}=1\\,\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478327 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478402 1069 "q-134039" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478402 1071 "Of the $3$ liters of strawberry juice Mark bought, he gave $\\frac{2}{5}$ liters to his niece Rose. As a mixed number, Mark has$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of strawberry juice left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478402 1072 #uuid "568d8931-746a-5ddb-bec0-b2b44679e4f4" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478402 1074 "To find out how much strawberry juice Mark has left, we must calculate the difference $3-\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{2}{5}$ from $3,$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $5.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{15}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{15-2}{5}=\\frac{13}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n13÷5=2\\,\\text{R}\\,3=2\\,\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mark has $2\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ liters of strawberry juice left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478312 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478403 1069 "q-106833" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478403 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478403 1072 #uuid "56e10a76-ac14-5a14-867b-2f5800a253a9" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478403 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478303 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478404 1069 "q-106837" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478404 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478404 1072 #uuid "594ccf89-70a6-5585-abdb-a8de137eebcb" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478404 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=-\\frac{1}{3},{u}_{33}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478248 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478405 1069 "q-149614" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478405 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(x+2y), & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{17}{30},$ find $\\text{E}[X+2Y].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478405 1072 #uuid "5aba2c2c-b7e8-572a-a813-c98db854f118" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478405 1074 "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $1\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{5}(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[xy+{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{2(x+1)}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{8}{3}+2-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+2Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+2\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{15}+2(\\frac{17}{30}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{15}+\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478298 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478406 1069 "q-32687" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478406 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478406 1072 #uuid "5eb23883-5996-5989-bf16-b8b58e00afb0" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478406 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478276 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478407 1069 "q-337239" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478407 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=(2{x}^{2}-1){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478407 1072 #uuid "605f7436-db66-5e71-900c-b1554cd28ac4" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478407 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478251 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478408 1069 "q-100720" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478408 1071 "![](images/6c/6cdbde09d3f5f1965b6de9798116331513bc3a7ee00a5784a5934eefbec0ed42.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{3}{4}$ given the model above." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478408 1072 #uuid "615e7b46-802a-503e-b549-f33b6cd0168c" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478408 1074 "The denominators are $5$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $5\\times 4=20.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $5$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/d4/d456f6fe7acc94b5a9e8e366fb2faea3ec2683e731a5e4cdafcdcbf6ce685bd2.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{20}÷\\frac{15}{20}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n12÷15=\\frac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{12}{15}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478265 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478291 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478348 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1069 "q-337387" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1072 #uuid "6273365a-c573-57e8-b4f1-3ec205edd4d2" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478409 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}\\int {e}^{4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478367 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478410 1069 "q-337236" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478410 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478410 1072 #uuid "6454caa7-3386-5602-a33c-711c745ca8a2" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478410 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478326 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478411 1069 "q-140541" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478411 1071 "The rate $r(t)$ at which the balance in a particular savings account grows is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=20{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of dollars per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years.\n\nTo the nearest thousand dollars, how much did the account's balance increase between years $t=2$ and $t=4?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478411 1072 #uuid "6686da9c-8c33-5aec-9383-87dd4a554b8f" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478411 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $$46\\,000.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478252 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478309 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478320 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478333 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478338 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478343 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478352 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478354 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1069 "q-333303" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.18$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2f/2f56bfb6777e6d95dfd887a525ea69a58725fc4f1a81bea84ebde67a72812518.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c9158f2818c1630debba65361b9be35a583d74591e389728528ffc3409ed89b.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1072 #uuid "6a4095b6-ac86-5129-b614-5959c7d2c664" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478412 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.18$ | $0$ | $1.36$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $1$ | $2.72$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2f/2f56bfb6777e6d95dfd887a525ea69a58725fc4f1a81bea84ebde67a72812518.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c9158f2818c1630debba65361b9be35a583d74591e389728528ffc3409ed89b.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478281 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478413 1069 "q-92662" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478413 1071 "![](images/14/14f6306057655315d9de48645c14c25c839f322500dcf599911c4b8d9deb637b.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{12}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{9}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478413 1072 #uuid "6c24b98e-30b0-587e-a562-0c053d2d0ee7" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478413 1074 "The denominators are $12$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $12$ by splitting the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/1d/1d05074d23c48a2ff9604d5a1aec5521c0fb043ebeed9424f7182e480404771d.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{12}÷\\frac{9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n7÷9=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{12}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,7\\,}}{9}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $7.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478335 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478414 1069 "q-143343" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478414 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478414 1072 #uuid "6ef50eeb-da62-5943-922a-49e7f6ad7489" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478414 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478268 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478415 1069 "q-349971" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478415 1071 "The rate at which customers enter a store one afternoon is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=6{e}^{t}+3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in customers per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{p.m}.$\nCustomers are also leaving the store at a steady rate of $5$ customers per hour.\n\nIf there were $40$ customers in the store at $1\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many customers will be there at $4\\,\\text{p.m}?$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, there will be \n{{field-1}}  customers in the store at $4\\,\\text{p.m}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478415 1072 #uuid "70deaba5-9010-5ebe-8348-672c8fc97ab0" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478415 1074 "Customers are entering the store at a rate of $6{e}^{t}+3$ customers per hour and are leaving at a rate of $5$ customers per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of customers (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-5 \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}+3-5 \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of customers** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{e}^{t}-2)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of packages after $3$ hours ($4\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=3$ into the above and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6{e}^{t}-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+(6{e}^{t}-2t){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =40+[(6{e}^{3}-2(3))-(6{e}^{0}-2(0))] \\\\  & =40+6{e}^{3}-12 \\\\  & =6{e}^{3}+28 \\\\  & ≈149\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, there will be $149$ customers in the store at $4\\,\\text{p.m}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478301 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478416 1069 "q-90730" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478416 1071 "People enter the ballpark at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n\n$$\n10t+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per minute,  and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the ballpark gates were opened to the public.  \n\nHow many people entered the ballpark between $t=10$ minutes and $t=20$  minutes." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478416 1072 #uuid "722ff44e-d1e5-509c-bcea-de9f4cf913cf" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478416 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** of the number of people $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of people who entered the ballpark between  $t=10$ minutes and $t=20$ minutes,  we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{20}10x+5\\,\\text{d}t & =5({x}^{2}+x){|}_{10}^{20} \\\\  & =5[({20}^{2}+20)-({10}^{2}+10)] \\\\  & =5\\cdot (420-110) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (310) \\\\  & =1\\,550\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=10$ minute and $t=20$ minutes,  a total of $1\\,550$ people entered the ballpark." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478293 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478417 1069 "q-40854" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478417 1071 "$(1+3)^{2}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478417 1072 #uuid "72d8229d-fbfb-5134-99e0-ea4a21f1b19e" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478417 1074 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(1+3)}_{⏟}}_{4}{}^{2}={4}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n{4}^{2}=16\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478341 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478418 1069 "q-106770" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478418 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478418 1072 #uuid "7301bd89-49bc-52af-80e0-064f8b80e2ca" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478418 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478360 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478419 1069 "q-196294" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478419 1071 "Expressed as a **mixed number** in lowest terms, $\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{1}{3}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478419 1072 #uuid "7779f547-8847-58f6-adc5-4d88825fc5bf" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478419 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{1}{3}$ is $\\frac{3}{1}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{4\\times 3}{5\\times 1}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{5}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478328 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478420 1069 "q-140845" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478420 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all distinct eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u$ is parallel to $v.$\n\nIII. $A$ is diagonalizable." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478420 1072 #uuid "78ca88fc-6dc6-5bfd-8b4b-e3a86c40d462" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478420 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. Since the eigenvectors are nonzero, we can't have $u$ parallel to $v.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478362 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478421 1069 "q-143375" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478421 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478421 1072 #uuid "7b80fc5d-3cc1-506f-9e31-9f2ad06b5cc7" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478421 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{4}{\\pi })}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478282 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478422 1069 "q-92767" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478422 1071 "$({5}^{2}-1)÷8=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478422 1072 #uuid "83c4d639-4eaa-5ee4-8602-bfbf3a10a0f6" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478422 1074 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses: $({5}^{2}-1).$\n\n To evaluate $({5}^{2}-1)$ we should also apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{25}{\\overset{⏞}{{5}^{2}}}-1=25-1\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n25-1=24\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n{{({5}^{2}-1)}_{⏟}}_{24}\\,÷8=24÷\\,8\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n\n$$\n24÷8=3\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478285 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478423 1069 "q-143355" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478423 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,0\\le y, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478423 1072 #uuid "8523bc2d-4a4c-583b-88c5-0e1cec2367ba" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478423 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x-y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{b}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478294 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478424 1069 "q-143367" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478424 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+2), & \\,1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478424 1072 #uuid "85b259be-6bf2-5d1d-9133-ee3e5619e62d" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478424 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,3]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(2+4)-\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{1}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{5}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478246 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478425 1069 "q-100747" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478425 1071 "${(-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478425 1072 #uuid "8b7a90d0-eb05-5b06-9c08-22e60340af71" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478425 1074 "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{9}=\\frac{(-1)}{9}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $2$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $2:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}={(\\frac{(-1)}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-1)^{2}=1$ and ${9}^{2}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{81}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478364 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478426 1069 "q-212255" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478426 1071 "$(1+5)^{2}+{4}^{3}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478426 1072 #uuid "8c874a34-8c6f-5943-b20d-99a608c0c078" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478426 1074 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(1+5)}_{⏟}}_{6}{}^{2}+{4}^{3}={6}^{2}+{4}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overset{36}{\\overset{⏞}{{6}^{2}}}+\\overset{64}{\\overset{⏞}{{4}^{3}}}=36+64\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n36+64=100\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478300 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478427 1069 "q-143581" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478427 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $\\text{E}[5X-Y].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478427 1072 #uuid "9118031b-d926-55ed-8f7f-1b50c43555cf" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478427 1074 "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{y\\sqrt{x}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3}{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{3}(\\frac{2}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\cdot \\frac{2y}{9}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(9-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-Y] & =5\\,\\text{E}[X]+(-1)\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{5})+(-1)(2) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478275 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478428 1069 "q-61482" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478428 1071 "![](images/82/82ee6742675576eb252a624a1089b3f77f3bf9a4db3be00798799f5380150173.png)\n\nCalculate $\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{6}$ using the model above." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478428 1072 #uuid "92d12b02-6a27-52b9-bae0-b7994bcddd77" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478428 1074 "The denominators are $4$ and $6.$ We can make a common denominator of $12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $3$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $4$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/2b/2b342d9977254cd8af8fb93fe632f4c154b6f797e3fcb89bdfc91945f7736dd1.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{12}÷\\frac{6}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n9÷6=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{6}=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478355 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478429 1069 "q-143561" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478429 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478429 1072 #uuid "9b844eb7-526d-53c8-86e0-b25751033892" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478429 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $…$ | $…$ | $…$ | $…$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the probability mass function for  $X$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.45$ | $0.55$ |\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.45+1\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =0+0.55 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478254 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478264 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478269 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478280 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478283 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478286 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478346 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478366 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1069 "q-333302" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.72$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $2.72$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1f/1f1d0d467285e26c4fc2f609c70d80fbb88867b77cdeb32c2a829006cbccea8e.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f7413babbb41aff9a892593d73c257d01ff24f2441f169e6089773b23434a5ab.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1072 #uuid "a814509c-4749-52dd-b5dc-eeb409609ce5" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478430 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc90697da27e52e0c1818d5529247d132944f30603936bedc191c4a14cb7d9c.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-t} \\\\ y={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2.72$ | $0$ | $0.30$ |\n| $y$ | $2.72$ | $1$ | $0.61$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1f/1f1d0d467285e26c4fc2f609c70d80fbb88867b77cdeb32c2a829006cbccea8e.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f7413babbb41aff9a892593d73c257d01ff24f2441f169e6089773b23434a5ab.png)" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478256 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478431 1069 "q-143342" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478431 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(y+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478431 1072 #uuid "a9efe9c4-330f-5e24-830e-56ad2ba97826" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478431 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}(y+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478325 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478432 1069 "q-134037" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478432 1071 "If $8$ people made all the muffins for the sale, and each made the same number of muffins, then each person made{{field-1}}\nmuffins." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478432 1072 #uuid "ad2f9bd5-68e3-5ec1-9893-522d4b4c3aca" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478432 1074 "We know that there are $336$ muffins in total. So, each person made $336÷8$ muffins.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}3\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $3$ (the number of hundreds in $336$) is less than the divisor $8.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}3\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $33÷8=4\\,\\text{R}\\,1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $8\\times 4=32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $33-32=1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}3\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $16÷8=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over ones: $2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $8\\times 2=16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $16-16=0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe get the remainder $0$, so the division is done.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n336÷8=42.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478305 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478433 1069 "q-143564" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478433 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478433 1072 #uuid "adc77faf-eae7-5c2f-8af1-752f3d590d9a" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478433 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $…$ | $…$ | $…$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the probability mass function for  $X$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =-0.3+0.4+0.9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478279 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478434 1069 "q-140545" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478434 1071 "The rate at which the number of visitors at a particular festival changes is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=4t+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of people per hour, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nHow many thousands of people did the number of visitors increase by between hours $t=1$ and $t=3?$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478434 1072 #uuid "b00e0f7a-d41e-5bb8-bfda-7014077ce7a5" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478434 1074 "We're given a **rate of change** in the number of visitors $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of visitors between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4t+2)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(2{t}^{2}+2t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(3)^{2}+2(3))-(2(1)^{2}+2(1)) \\\\  & =18+6-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between hours $t=1$ and $t=3,$ the number of visitors increased by $20$ thousand people." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478274 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478435 1069 "q-75105" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478435 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478435 1072 #uuid "b3806686-48f6-58e2-b9ed-3e1024804191" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478435 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478334 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478436 1069 "q-92736" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478436 1071 "$\\frac{1}{21}÷\\frac{2}{3}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478436 1072 #uuid "b712bd99-fd9d-578f-99be-b3722982cb29" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478436 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{3}$ is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{21}÷\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{1}{21}\\times \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{21}\\times \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1\\times 3}{21\\times 2}=\\frac{3}{42}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{42}=\\frac{3÷3}{42÷3}=\\frac{1}{14}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478358 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478437 1069 "q-62202" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478437 1071 "$(2+3)-{2}^{2}=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478437 1072 #uuid "b722908d-85fd-55c4-bb73-d86e9a694c75" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478437 1074 "We use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n{{(2+3)}_{⏟}}_{5}-{2}^{2}=5-{2}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we find the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n5\\,-\\overset{4}{\\overset{⏞}{{2}^{2}}}=5\\,-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **S**ubtraction:\n\n$$\n5-4=1\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478263 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478277 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478363 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1069 "q-352902" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1072 #uuid "b7565b02-4af8-5778-b268-0a737a2704ef" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478438 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478316 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478439 1069 "q-143380" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478439 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478439 1072 #uuid "b7f5fbd8-1657-5933-82eb-1254d5fe2e87" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478439 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{x}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478318 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478440 1069 "q-143571" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478440 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[2X-3Y].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478440 1072 #uuid "b990b80d-8f23-5220-a540-72805037f053" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478440 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,  the probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.35+2\\cdot 0.35+4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.35+0.7+1.2 \\\\  & =2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.6+3\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2+1.2 \\\\  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-3Y] & =2\\,\\text{E}[X]-3\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2(2.25)-3(2.4) \\\\  & =4.5-7.2 \\\\  & =-2.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478349 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478441 1069 "q-97861" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478441 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $C?$\n\nI. ${\\lambda }^{4}+2{\\lambda }^{3}+3{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda =0$\n\nII. $\\lambda (\\lambda -2)^{2}(\\lambda +3)=0$\n\nIII. ${\\lambda }^{4}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+4=0$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478441 1072 #uuid "bc0c0d74-29c3-5a54-b40b-f1888e3dbce2" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478441 1074 "Since $C$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could  be the characteristic equation of $C.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478249 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478288 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478295 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1069 "q-337516" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y={e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1072 #uuid "bcdda2fe-c778-5147-ad87-b07c607973b4" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478442 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+6(u{e}^{-6x}) & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-6u{e}^{-6x}+6u{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478266 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478443 1069 "q-134036" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478443 1071 "At the sale, there are $14$ tables. If there are $24$ muffins on each table, then there are{{field-1}}\nmuffins in total." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478443 1072 #uuid "bdb9c372-6491-56bd-a5e0-6ea4e60521c8" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478443 1074 "To calculate the total number of muffins, we need to multiply $14$ by $24.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\times 4=56\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\times 20=280\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}56+280=336\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 24=336.$\n\nTherefore, there are $336$ muffins in total." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478314 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478444 1069 "q-143345" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478444 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478444 1072 #uuid "bdd3bac4-4f21-5e93-9c5a-1e5e329b0b34" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478444 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478270 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478445 1069 "q-98666" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478445 1071 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=1$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478445 1072 #uuid "bee1b915-a838-5542-8a64-106e980bc53d" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478445 1074 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478330 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478339 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1069 "q-349995" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1072 #uuid "c581124e-b7b1-5b7e-bfb5-1ff3fee7303f" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478446 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(8-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3(8-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =25+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+3{[8x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =25+3[(8\\cdot 4-{4}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =25+3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $25<73$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $73$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478317 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478447 1069 "q-222766" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478447 1071 "![](images/d9/d9884bf61d9b904b3e6846e6a1d307e34e1b84023e8e5a321c09be6bfd44069c.png)\n\nUse the model above to compute the following result. Express your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n$\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478447 1072 #uuid "c9451a4b-084f-5269-a43e-e06464c25eb9" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478447 1074 "![](images/32/327c59257b6ea1d0b18f73bcff065a8ddade49db9ce6b84ba44c824d1c041901.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{3}{5}$ fits into $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $3$ fits into $1.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}$, we can simply compute $1÷3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n1÷3=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478340 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478448 1069 "q-61604" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478448 1071 "![](images/6b/6b5c4781d474dffc32a48a6d9b814e8181dd73b596ba05828d918fa42a3916ab.png)\n\nCalculate $\\frac{7}{10}÷\\frac{3}{5}$ using the model above." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478448 1072 #uuid "cab23e35-9f7a-52cc-9e73-2a48af990f9d" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478448 1074 "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/43/43424ef31fc44fa8485ee7a7b5a0ce6855858ad477d8365ac2ad898d25353f29.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{10}÷\\frac{6}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n7÷6=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{10}÷\\frac{3}{5}=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478302 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478329 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478347 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1069 "q-337383" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-2.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1072 #uuid "cb143e45-7802-56af-9fb3-105326be7da5" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478449 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+6(u{e}^{-6x}) & =-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-6u{e}^{-6x}+6u{e}^{-6x} & =-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}} & =-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x} & =-2 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =-2{e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =-2{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6x}\\cdot {e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478290 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478450 1069 "q-346832" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478450 1071 "Environmental scientists are studying the population of fish in a lake.\nThey observe that the fish population increases due to reproduction at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=40{t}^{2}+6t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in fish per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the study began.\nAt the same time, $10$ fish are lost each month due to predation.\n\nIf there were initially $500$ fish in the lake, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of fish in the lake $t$ months after the beginning of the study.\n\n$P(t)=\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478450 1072 #uuid "d1f89285-0002-5f93-8ef8-535c491c51d1" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478450 1074 "The population of the lake is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ fish per month and is decreasing at a rate of $10$ fish per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of fish is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-10 \\\\  & =40{t}^{2}+6t-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of fish after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(40{x}^{2}+6x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}40{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{t}6x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =500+{40\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}+{6\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{t}-10x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =500+\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-10t \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-10t+500\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478272 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478451 1069 "q-143580" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478451 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find $\\text{E}[X+Y].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478451 1072 #uuid "dc0d3274-ca1e-5ab4-bb51-ab607148446e" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478451 1074 "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478287 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478452 1069 "q-327994" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478452 1071 "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478452 1072 #uuid "dc2a00c1-69ff-5ca9-838c-e411473b3c5e" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478452 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(4)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478336 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478453 1069 "q-75110" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478453 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation \n${R}_{1}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478453 1072 #uuid "e13146e0-974e-5b74-8d7d-df8fb20c3089" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478453 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478306 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478454 1069 "q-97971" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478454 1071 "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $B+3I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478454 1072 #uuid "e4997f39-9351-5840-b1b4-1804fe6c9f0e" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478454 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478262 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478455 1069 "q-36429" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478455 1071 "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1\\},\\,∂B=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n\nII. The point $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ belongs to $B$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,-1)$ is an interior point of $B$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478455 1072 #uuid "e5330869-80f9-574d-be15-bcaa4f259731" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478455 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/97/97ded2e99adb83b69bc547cef822c46ac2134d3ed17c9cdf65f24083560afae5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $Q(0,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/38/38a11d6712843367b08ff98843b3c3daf43729d82adbe70e15b8019fb68cee6c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂B,$ the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1.$ Hence, $P$ does not belongs to $B.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478308 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478456 1069 "q-30579" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478456 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478456 1072 #uuid "e5e4ccfa-82ff-5c85-afb9-eb5972468216" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478456 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =1 \\\\ R & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478359 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478457 1069 "q-222987" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478457 1071 "![](images/21/21d3c3c28f9ef10e5ac2623724ead9593ba24dbb1f94b81ce5313f94ee7ca511.png)\n\nUse the model above to compute the following result. Express your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n$\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{6}{7}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478457 1072 #uuid "e84b31c0-fab3-5cf9-b831-412b917ad955" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478457 1074 "The denominators are $4$ and $7.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 7=28.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $7$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $4$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/91/919b2e3f87aabf375cb968e876ee19b0ce9cd6a70c41088b062459a1f4f1f133.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{28}÷\\frac{24}{28}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n21÷24=\\frac{21}{24}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{6}{7}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478311 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478458 1069 "q-143379" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478458 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y{e}^{-3x},\\, & 0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478458 1072 #uuid "eac65ba4-c3fd-5292-817a-bbb6eafdc1fe" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478458 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478260 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478315 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1069 "q-134040" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1071 "After Rose takes her share of strawberry juice, Mark's remaining amount of strawberry juice is{{field-1}}\nthan the remaining amount of orange juice because $2\\,\\frac{3}{5}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{7}{4}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1072 #uuid "edc909fc-f87e-5b26-96f1-797620d48727" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478459 1074 "Mark has $2\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ liters of strawberry juice and $\\frac{7}{4}$ liters of orange juice remaining.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $5\\times 4=20.$\n\nTo put $2\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ over a denominator of $20,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{13}{5}=\\frac{13\\times 4}{5\\times 4}=\\frac{52}{20}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{7}{4}$ over a denominator of $20,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{7\\times 5}{4\\times 5}=\\frac{35}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{52}{20}$ and $\\frac{35}{20}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $52>35,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{52}{20}>\\frac{35}{20}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Rose takes her part of strawberry juice, Mark's remaining amount of strawberry juice is $>$ than the remaining amount of orange juice because $2\\,\\frac{3}{5}>\\frac{7}{4}.$\"" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478324 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478460 1069 "q-196268" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478460 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form or as an integer, $\\frac{1}{8}÷\\frac{1}{40}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478460 1072 #uuid "f566cc7e-16bb-505a-9e70-89d88e0f6bda" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478460 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{1}{40}$ is $\\frac{40}{1}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{8}÷\\frac{1}{40}=\\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{40}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{40}{1}=\\frac{1\\times 40}{8\\times 1}=\\frac{40}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n40÷8=5\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478259 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478461 1069 "q-110459" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478461 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478461 1072 #uuid "f636a770-dab5-5ae4-9692-f47eb622812f" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478461 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of ${I}_{3}$ and adding $4$ times the third row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3}$ to $A.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478273 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478462 1069 "q-196291" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478462 1071 "Expressed as a **mixed number** in lowest terms, $\\frac{9}{10}÷\\frac{3}{4}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478462 1072 #uuid "f72c51cd-c795-582a-a4d9-ceda93dea841" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478462 1074 "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{3}{4}$ is $\\frac{4}{3}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{10}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{9}{10}\\times \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{10}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{9\\times 4}{10\\times 3}=\\frac{36}{30}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{5}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{5}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478342 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478463 1069 "q-143582" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478463 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}, & -3\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ find $\\text{E}[3X+7Y].$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478463 1072 #uuid "faa5f199-79d7-5c93-85da-fb2ae026c8e7" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478463 1074 "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+xy]}_{x=-3}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+3y)-(\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}-3y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[18+6y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3y+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{11}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+7Y] & =3\\,\\text{E}[X]+7\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3(0)+7(\\frac{11}{21}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478297 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478464 1069 "q-99282" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478464 1071 "Find the value of $\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}.$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478464 1072 #uuid "fb327d68-ea77-517c-9e6a-fcce87fc3351" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478464 1074 "First, we rewrite the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}={(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}})}^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478278 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478465 1069 "q-274555" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478465 1071 "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478465 1072 #uuid "fb921c8c-f3ea-56c4-a4fb-c6ad860350a8" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478465 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478292 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478466 1069 "q-349763" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478466 1071 "A factory is tracking the rate at which plastic pellets are produced. The rate $r(t)$ at which pellets are produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=0.4\\sqrt{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in kilograms per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} kilograms were produced." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478466 1072 #uuid "fdea6428-2f62-5007-88f6-e327c0da6446" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478466 1074 "We're given the **rate of change** in the amount of plastic pellets produced, $r(t).$ To find the amount of plastic pellets produced between the times $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}0.4\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={0.4\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & ={\\frac{0.8}{3}{t}^{3/2}\\,|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.8}{3}(10)^{3/2}-\\frac{0.8}{3}(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{10}}{3}-\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & ≈8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, $8.2$ kilograms were produced." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478319 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478467 1069 "q-134038" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478467 1071 "Mark bought $3$ liters of strawberry juice and $\\frac{7}{4}$ liters of orange juice. How much juice did Mark buy in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nMark bought$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of juice." 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478467 1072 #uuid "ff575d67-9751-56f2-8a79-bde7f1b08899" 13194139535179 17592186045417 true] [17592186478467 1074 "To find out how much juice Mark bought in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n3+\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add $3$ and $\\frac{7}{4},$ we need to express the whole number $3$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $4.$\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{12+7}{4}=\\frac{19}{4}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as